Now I understand the struct layout. The `Packet` struct has:
- Version-Major (bit[4])
- Version-Minor (bit[4])
- Packet-Type (uint8)
- Sequence-Number (uint8)
- Flags (uint8)
- Session-Id (uint32)
- Length (uint32)

That's 12 bytes total (the header). So `fr_struct_from_network` decodes the 12-byte header. Then in the switch, it accesses the body.

Now, the key issue. Let me think about the global-buffer-overflow READ 1. 

Let me reconsider `fr_struct_from_network`. It's called with `buffer` and `buffer_len`. The struct decoder processes the header fields. For the bit fields (Version-Major, Version-Minor), it reads bytes. Then Packet-Type, Sequence-Number, Flags, Session-Id, Length.

Wait, but the struct decoder is called with the FULL buffer (buffer_len), not just the header. So it decodes the header fields, then since there's no key field (key_vp is NULL because none of the header fields are key fields), it goes to `done` and returns `data_len`.

Hmm, but actually the struct decoder reads the header fields from the buffer. The header is 12 bytes. If buffer_len >= 12, that's fine.

Let me reconsider. The global-buffer-overflow READ 1. Let me look at the `fr_tacacs_decode` switch statement again. After `fr_struct_from_network`, it accesses `pkt->hdr.type`. But wait - `pkt` points to `buffer`. And `buffer_len >= 12` was checked. So `pkt->hdr.type` is at offset 1, within bounds.

Let me look at the `packet_is_authen_start_request` macro: `p->hdr.seq_no == 1`. That's at offset 2. Fine.

Let me look at the body access. `p = BODY(authen.start)` = `buffer + 12 + sizeof(fr_tacacs_packet_authen_start_hdr_t)`. The authen_start_hdr is 8 bytes. So p = buffer + 20.

Then `PACKET_HEADER_CHECK("Authentication Start")` checks `p > end`. If buffer_len < 20, then p > end and it goes to fail. Good.

Then it accesses `pkt->authen.start.authen_type` etc. These are within the 8-byte body header. But wait - the check `PACKET_HEADER_CHECK` only checks that `p <= end`, i.e., buffer_len >= 20. But accessing `pkt->authen.start.authen_type` requires buffer_len >= 12 + 8 = 20. So that's fine.

Hmm. Let me reconsider. Let me look at the `DECODE_FIELD_STRING8` macro:

```c
#define DECODE_FIELD_STRING8(_da, _field) do { \
        if (tacacs_decode_field(ctx, cursor, _da, &p, \
            _field, end) < 0) goto fail; \
} while (0)
```

And `tacacs_decode_field`:
```c
if ((p + field_len) > end) { ... return -1; }
```

That checks bounds. OK.

Let me reconsider. The global-buffer-overflow READ 1. Let me look at the `fr_tacacs_body_xor` function more carefully.

```c
pad_offset = sizeof(pkt->hdr.session_id) + secret_len + sizeof(pkt->hdr.version) + sizeof(pkt->hdr.seq_no);
buf = talloc_array(NULL, uint8_t, pad_offset + MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH);
memcpy(&buf[0], &pkt->hdr.session_id, sizeof(pkt->hdr.session_id));
memcpy(&buf[sizeof(pkt->hdr.session_id)], secret, secret_len);
memcpy(&buf[sizeof(pkt->hdr.session_id) + secret_len], &pkt->hdr.version, sizeof(pkt->hdr.version));
memcpy(&buf[sizeof(pkt->hdr.session_id) + secret_len + sizeof(pkt->hdr.version)], &pkt->hdr.seq_no, sizeof(pkt->hdr.seq_no));
```

This reads from `pkt->hdr.session_id`, `pkt->hdr.version`, `pkt->hdr.seq_no`. These are within the header (12 bytes). Fine.

Then:
```c
size_t pos = 0;
do {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < MD5_DIGEST_LENGTH && pos < body_len; i++, pos++)
        body[pos] ^= pad[i];
    ...
} while (1);
```

`body = decrypted + sizeof(pkt->hdr)`, `body_len = length = ntohl(pkt->hdr.length)`. Since `buffer_len = 12 + length`, `body_len = buffer_len - 12`. So `body[pos]` for pos in [0, body_len) is within `decrypted + 12 + body_len = decrypted + buffer_len`. Fine.

Hmm. Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is in the `fr_struct_from_network` bit field handling. Let me look at the bit field code:

```c
if (da_is_bit_field(child)) {
    uint8_t array[8];
    unsigned int num_bits;
    uint64_t value;

    num_bits = offset + child->flags.length;
    if ((end - p) < fr_bytes_from_bits(num_bits)) goto unknown;

    memset(array, 0, sizeof(array));
    memcpy(&array[0], p, fr_bytes_from_bits(num_bits));
    ...
}
```

For Version-Major (bit[4]) and Version-Minor (bit[4]), these are bit fields. `fr_bytes_from_bits(4)` = 1. So it reads 1 byte. Fine.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Let me look at the actual OSS-Fuzz issue. Let me search online.

